# Task 9 (+ final whole-branch review FIX 1/2/3): the `location` blocks the
# real VPS's PUBLIC nginx needs for `/live/`, re-authorising EVERY HLS
# request against apps/api instead of trusting MediaMTX's own read-auth
# cache (which authorises once per viewer session, not once per request —
# see mediamtx-webhooks.ts's `/auth-request` docstring and
# task-9-report.md for the empirical finding this responds to) — AND, as of
# the final whole-branch review, rewriting the PUBLIC path (which now
# carries only an event id, never the stream key) onto MediaMTX's
# unchanged INTERNAL path (which still carries the stream key) after that
# re-authorisation succeeds.
#
# THIS IS A FRAGMENT, NOT A STANDALONE nginx SERVER — it is `location`
# blocks, meant to be pasted (or `include`d, after rendering the one
# templated value below) INSIDE the real public HTTPS server block that
# already serves this app's SPA and API paths on :443. It is NOT its own
# `server { listen ...; }` — the real deployment has exactly one public
# nginx front door, not a second listener on a second port. (An earlier
# version of this file WAS a standalone `server { listen 8443; }` — that
# was this task's own local Docker-based proof harness, committed by
# mistake as if it were the deployable artifact. Fixed: this file is now
# the thing meant to actually ship.)
#
# FIX 2 (final whole-branch review, Important): "fixed" above was true of
# the FRAGMENT's own contents, but nobody had actually PROVEN the shipped
# file — copied verbatim into a real `server {}` block, reloaded, driven
# with real requests — works. The Task 9 behavioural proof used a
# DIFFERENT artifact (the standalone 8443 harness with
# host.docker.internal targets), verified via `nginx -t`, which is SYNTAX,
# not behaviour. The final whole-branch review's own verification instead
# built a real `server { listen 8443 ssl; ... include
# live-hls.conf.template; }` (self-signed cert, real MediaMTX, real
# apps/api, envsubst-rendered exactly like a real deploy would) and drove
# the identical checks THROUGH that server block — see
# `final-fix-report.md` for the transcript. Only after that did this file
# get treated as proven.
#
# THE ONE TEMPLATED VALUE: `${MEDIAMTX_WEBHOOK_SECRET}`. Render it with
# whatever secrets-injection mechanism the real deploy uses before
# `include`ing this file — plain `envsubst 'MEDIAMTX_WEBHOOK_SECRET' <
# live-hls.conf.template > /etc/nginx/snippets/live-hls.conf` is enough for
# a bare-VPS nginx (the documented deployment — see below). If this nginx
# is ever run inside a container instead (NOT how this project documents
# it today), the official nginx image's own `docker-entrypoint.d`
# templating works too, but ONLY with `NGINX_ENVSUBST_FILTER=
# MEDIAMTX_WEBHOOK_SECRET` explicitly set — an unfiltered envsubst replaces
# every `$name`-shaped token in this file from the process environment,
# which would silently blank out nginx's OWN variables below
# (`$request_uri`, `$host`, `$mtx_event`, `$mtx_key`, ...) the moment any of
# those names ALSO happened to match an environment variable.
#
# THE DOCUMENTED DEPLOYMENT IS BARE-METAL, NOT CONTAINERISED — this is why
# both `proxy_pass` targets below are `127.0.0.1`, not a Docker hostname.
# nginx itself is not a service in `infra/docker-compose.yml` at all (that
# file only runs Postgres and MediaMTX); it runs directly on the VPS host,
# same as `apps/api`. From there, `127.0.0.1:8888` correctly reaches
# MediaMTX's HLS port (docker-compose maps it to the HOST's own loopback,
# not to a container-network address), and `127.0.0.1:3000` correctly
# reaches `apps/api` (also running directly on the host, not containerised
# — there is no `api` service in docker-compose.yml to be one).
#
# IF YOU EVER CONTAINERISE THIS nginx INSTEAD (not the documented path,
# but worth recording since it changes both proxy_pass targets): a
# container's own loopback is not the host's. This task's own local proof
# needed nginx running as a Docker container (there is no other way to
# stand up nginx in this project's sandboxed dev environment), and BOTH
# `127.0.0.1` targets below had to become the container's view of the host
# — `host.docker.internal`'s resolved IPv4 address specifically, because a
# `resolver`-dependent hostname lookup for `host.docker.internal` failed
# without one, and the container used was not on a Docker-managed network
# with the embedded DNS server that resolver needs (see task-9-report.md
# for the exact failure and the working substitution). This is a real,
# tested variant, but it is NOT what ships — recorded here only so a future
# change to a containerised nginx does not have to rediscover it.
#
# OPERATIONAL COUPLING THIS INTRODUCES, WORTH KNOWING BEFORE DEPLOYING:
# every single HLS request now blocks on a live round trip to `apps/api`.
# An `apps/api` restart (a deploy, a crash, `bun --hot` picking up a file
# change) now stalls every in-flight viewer for as long as the timeouts
# below allow, where before Task 9 an API restart had NO effect on
# already-playing viewers at all (MediaMTX's own cache kept serving them).
# This is the intended trade — see task-9-report.md §1 for why the
# alternative (MediaMTX's own per-session cache) is a real security gap —
# but it is a genuine new coupling, not a free change.

# FIX 3 (final whole-branch review, Important): "/webhooks/mediamtx/ is
# never on the public surface" was, until this block existed, a claim
# about what THIS file does not contain — never a guard against whatever
# ELSE the same server block proxies. `/webhooks/xendit` and
# `/webhooks/telegram` MUST be publicly reachable (Xendit and Telegram both
# call them from the internet), so the ordinary way to wire that is a
# single `location /webhooks/ { proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:3000; }` — and
# `/webhooks/mediamtx/*` sits directly under that same prefix. Without an
# explicit guard, `POST /webhooks/mediamtx/auth?secret=...` and
# `GET /webhooks/mediamtx/auth-request` become PUBLICLY REACHABLE by
# default, writing `MEDIAMTX_WEBHOOK_SECRET` into apps/api's access log on
# every call (the secret travels as a query parameter on `/auth` — see
# that route's own docstring for why) and letting a stranger probe the
# `/auth`/`/auth-request`/`/lifecycle` endpoints directly.
#
# `location ^~ /webhooks/mediamtx/` — a PREFIX location (not a regex),
# `deny all;` inside it. Two independent reasons this wins over whatever
# else the server block proxies, so ORDER IN THE FILE DOES NOT MATTER for
# this location the way it would for a `~` regex location (see the `/live/`
# location below for the case where it DOES matter and how this file
# avoids it):
#   1. `/webhooks/mediamtx/` (18 characters) is a LONGER, more specific
#      prefix than `/webhooks/` (10 characters) or `/` — nginx's own
#      location-selection rule picks the LONGEST matching prefix among
#      plain prefix locations, independent of `^~` and independent of
#      where either location appears in the file.
#   2. `^~` additionally means: once this is the longest matching prefix,
#      nginx never even evaluates any REGEX (`~`/`~*`) location that might
#      otherwise have matched `/webhooks/mediamtx/...` with higher
#      priority than a plain `/webhooks/` prefix would.
# Together: no matter what a real deploy's existing `/webhooks/` (or a
# broader `/` SPA fallback) location looks like, or where in the file it
# is declared, this one wins for anything under `/webhooks/mediamtx/`.
# Paste this block ANYWHERE in the server block — the ordering note that
# matters in this file is on the `/live/` location below, not this one.
location ^~ /webhooks/mediamtx/ {
    deny all;
}

# Task 1 (browser-publishing phase): the public WHIP SIGNALLING endpoint —
# proxies to MediaMTX's webrtcAddress (8889, loopback-only — see
# infra/mediamtx.yml and infra/docker-compose.yml) over 443, exactly the
# way `/live/` below already does for HLS on port 8888. CONFIRMED against a
# live MediaMTX server, not assumed from docs:
# `http://<host>:8889/<path>/whip` is MediaMTX's actual WHIP ingest url (a
# browser POSTs its SDP offer there) — a DIFFERENT url from MediaMTX's own
# browser publish PAGE (`/<path>/publish`, an HTML page whose own
# JavaScript is what POSTs to `/whip`). See CONTRIBUTING.md's "Browser
# publishing (WebRTC / WHIP)" section for the full transcript this was
# verified against, including the real `201 Created` / SDP-answer body / ICE
# negotiation this location's shape was built to carry unmodified.
#
# `access_log off` — REQUIRED, per this task's brief: the stream key
# travels in this url's path (`/whip/<streamKey>`, or
# `/whip/<streamKey>/<sessionId>` for the ICE-trickle PATCH and the DELETE
# WHIP issues against the session MediaMTX creates), exactly like RTMP's
# `rtmpUrl` already does. A creator legitimately owns this credential (it
# comes back from `POST .../events` alongside `rtmpUrl` — this is the
# PUBLISH side, not the read side `/live/` below protects), but nginx's own
# access log on disk is still not the place for it to sit in plaintext, the
# identical reasoning `/live/`'s own `access_log off` below applies to the
# watch token.
#
# THE ERROR LOG CARRIES THIS KEY TOO, ON THE MOST ORDINARY FAILURE THERE IS
# — found running this for real, with MediaMTX stopped:
#   connect() failed (111: Connection refused) while connecting to upstream,
#   ... upstream: "http://127.0.0.1:8889/live/<streamKey>/whip"
# The stream key appears TWICE in that one line (the upstream URL nginx
# tried, and the original request URI nginx also logs alongside it) — once
# per failed attempt. Triggered by MediaMTX being unreachable, which
# `scripts/deploy.sh` causes on every single redeploy, however briefly, by
# restarting the MediaMTX container while nginx keeps running. `access_log
# off` above keeps this out of the ACCESS log exactly as designed; it does
# nothing for the ERROR log, same as `/live/`'s own `auth_request` failure
# below — see that location's note for the full reasoning on why this
# project accepts that trade (an operator needing the error log when things
# break) rather than raising nginx's error-log threshold to suppress it.
#
# NO `auth_request` HERE, deliberately — unlike `/live/` below. A publish is
# authorised by MediaMTX's OWN `authHTTPAddress` call straight to
# apps/api's `/webhooks/mediamtx/auth`, over `host.docker.internal`, never
# through this nginx at all (see infra/mediamtx.yml) — the SAME gate RTMP
# publishing already goes through, and the design's own explicit
# constraint: no second credential, no second auth path. This location's
# only job is carrying the WHIP signalling bytes across the one hop
# Cloudflare's TCP-only proxy can actually carry (nginx on 443) — the media
# itself never touches nginx at all, see webrtcLocalUDPAddress in
# infra/mediamtx.yml.
#
# WHY A SEPARATE `/whip/` PREFIX, NOT NESTED UNDER `/live/`: MediaMTX's own
# WHIP url shape is `/live/<streamKey>/whip[/<sessionId>]` — literally
# UNDER the `/live/` prefix the read location below already owns as a
# `^~` PREFIX location. Per nginx's own location-selection algorithm (see
# that location's own header comment for the full citation), a `^~` prefix
# location, once selected as the longest matching prefix, makes nginx skip
# EVERY regex location for that request — so a `~ ^/live/(.+)/whip$`
# location placed anywhere else in this file, in any order, would NEVER be
# reached for a request under `/live/`; `/live/`'s own `^~` status wins
# unconditionally. The only way to give WHIP requests different handling
# than HLS reads without touching that read location's security-critical
# `auth_request` logic at all is a prefix that does not literally start
# with "/live/" — "/whip/" doesn't (nginx's longest-PREFIX rule requires an
# exact literal-character match, and "/whip/" / "/live/" share no common
# prefix beyond "/"), so this location and `/live/` below never compete for
# the same request, and this file keeps the same "no regex, no ordering
# question" property the read location's own comments already establish.
#
# THE REWRITE, both directions: the public url is `/whip/<streamKey>` (or
# `/whip/<streamKey>/<sessionId>`) — one path segment SHORTER than
# MediaMTX's own `/live/<streamKey>/whip[/<sessionId>]`, since the fixed
# "whip" text moved from a middle segment to the prefix. `$whip_key`/
# `$whip_session` below capture the two dynamic pieces from the public url
# (mirroring `$mtx_event`/`$mtx_rest` in `/live/` below) and `proxy_pass`
# rebuilds MediaMTX's own shape from them. WHIP's own protocol is what makes
# the SECOND rewrite direction necessary: MediaMTX answers the initial POST
# with `201 Created` and a `Location` header naming the new session
# resource — confirmed with a real POST against a real server — and that
# header is MediaMTX echoing back the INTERNAL path it received
# (`/live/<streamKey>/whip/<sessionId>`), the same class of leak `/live/`'s
# own `proxy_redirect` below exists to fix for HLS's cookie-check redirect.
# `proxy_redirect` here does the identical job: rewrites that `Location`
# back to the public `/whip/<streamKey>/<sessionId>` shape before nginx
# returns it, so the browser's own WHIP client — which resolves `Location`
# against the url it already has — issues its later PATCH/DELETE calls back
# at THIS location instead of a url nginx never proxies.
location ^~ /whip/ {
    set $whip_key "";
    set $whip_session "";
    # $request_uri is the RAW request line — not percent-decoded — so this
    # capture (and the proxy_redirect below, which rebuilds MediaMTX's
    # internal path from the same $whip_key) is only correct as long as
    # $whip_key can never legitimately contain a character that would
    # decode differently from its raw form. True today because `newStreamKey`
    # (streaming-provider.port.ts) only ever mints 32-char lowercase hex, but
    # NOT enforced here — a future key format with, say, a literal `/` would
    # need re-checking this assumption, not just this comment updated.
    if ($request_uri ~ "^/whip/([^/?]+)(/[^/?]+)?") {
        set $whip_key $1;
        set $whip_session $2;
    }

    access_log off;

    # MediaMTX answers a REFUSED publish with `401` plus `WWW-Authenticate: Basic`,
    # because it also supports username/password auth as an alternative to the webhook
    # this project uses. Left alone, the browser sees that header come back from our
    # OWN origin and pops its native sign-in dialog — a credential prompt on
    # diudara.mhamzah.id that no username or password can satisfy, that looks exactly
    # like a phishing attempt, and that replaces the Indonesian explanation
    # `whip-publisher.ts` would otherwise show. Observed in production. Hiding the
    # header leaves a plain 401, so our own copy does the talking.
    proxy_hide_header WWW-Authenticate;

    proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8889/live/$whip_key/whip$whip_session$is_args$args;
    proxy_redirect /live/$whip_key/whip/ /whip/$whip_key/;
    proxy_set_header Host $host;
    proxy_http_version 1.1;
    proxy_set_header Connection "";
}

# FIX 1 (final whole-branch review, Critical) + FIX 2 (Important, the
# location-matching half): the PUBLIC path a member's browser ever
# requests is now `/live/<eventId>/...` — an opaque row id, never the
# stream key `MediaMTX` authorises publishes against (see
# `ResolveWatchToken`'s own docstring in apps/api for the full reasoning).
# MediaMTX's INTERNAL path is UNCHANGED (`live/<streamKey>`), so this
# location must REWRITE the request after `auth_request` succeeds, using
# the stream key the auth subrequest hands back — MediaMTX was never
# taught about event ids.
#
# `^~ /live/`, NOT a `~` regex location — this is FIX 2's other half.
# Regex (`~`) locations are evaluated in SOURCE ORDER, and the FIRST one
# that matches wins — not the most specific one. A pre-existing regex
# location elsewhere in this server block matching something as ordinary
# as `\.(m3u8|ts)$` (a plausible "cache my static/streaming assets"
# catch-all, or part of an SPA's own asset-serving rules) would, if it
# happened to be declared EARLIER in the file, intercept every HLS
# request with NO `auth_request` at all — a silent, total bypass of this
# entire fix, and nothing about `nginx -t` or a casual read of this file
# would reveal it. `^~` sidesteps the ordering question entirely rather
# than merely documenting it: prefix locations are found FIRST, tracking
# the LONGEST match, and if THAT longest-matching prefix location carries
# `^~`, nginx uses it immediately and — per nginx's own documented
# location-selection algorithm — NEVER EVALUATES ANY REGEX LOCATION AT
# ALL, regardless of source order or how those regexes are written. A
# `\.(m3u8|ts)$` regex anywhere else in the file, in any position, simply
# cannot intercept a `/live/...` request once this location exists — not
# "first one wins", but "the regex phase never runs" for this path. (This
# guarantee assumes no OTHER `^~` or exact-match `=` location shares the
# `/live/` prefix with equal or greater specificity — a safe assumption
# for a namespace this distinctive, and worth an explicit grep for `^~`
# and `location =` before pasting this in, same as any config change.)
#
# THE CAPTURE, WITHOUT A REGEX LOCATION: `^~` locations cannot carry named
# regex captures themselves (that syntax belongs to `~` locations only —
# the thing this file deliberately avoids). The event id and the rest of
# the request path are captured instead with an `if` block matching
# `$request_uri` — a well-known, if inelegant, nginx idiom for exactly
# this: getting regex captures inside a prefix location nginx will always
# reach. `if` here is safe (the usual "if is evil" warnings are about `if`
# combined with `return`/`rewrite ... last` producing surprising control
# flow; this `if` only ever runs `set`, which has none of those hazards).
location ^~ /live/ {
    set $mtx_event "";
    set $mtx_rest "";
    if ($request_uri ~ "^/live/([^/?]+)(/[^?]*)?") {
        set $mtx_event $1;
        set $mtx_rest $2;
    }

    # `$watch_token`, captured BEFORE `auth_request` fires — load-bearing
    # ordering, found running this for real (Task 9): nginx's
    # `auth_request` subrequest does NOT inherit `$args`/`$arg_*` from the
    # request it is authorising, but a plain `set` variable DOES persist
    # into the subrequest, the same way a regex capture does. See the
    # internal location below for the empirical trace.
    set $watch_token $arg_token;
    auth_request /_internal/mediamtx-auth-request;

    # Captures the auth subrequest's `X-Stream-Key` RESPONSE header into an
    # nginx variable — this is what lets a request that arrived as
    # `/live/<eventId>/...` get proxied to MediaMTX as
    # `/live/<streamKey>/...` below. `auth_request_set` is the ONLY way a
    # value from the auth subrequest's response reaches the rest of this
    # location; nginx does not forward an `auth_request` subrequest's
    # headers to anything on its own. If `auth_request` above refused
    # (401/403), nginx has already stopped processing this request before
    # reaching this line — `$mtx_key` is only ever read after a successful
    # authorisation.
    auth_request_set $mtx_key $upstream_http_x_stream_key;

    # nginx's default access log format includes the full request line —
    # `GET /live/<eventId>/index.m3u8?token=<...> HTTP/1.1` — which would
    # write the watch token to disk on every single request, a brand new
    # exposure this proxy would otherwise introduce (MediaMTX's own HLS
    # port was loopback-only with no public-facing access log at all
    # before Task 9). Off, for the same reason `/webhooks/mediamtx/auth`'s
    # query-string secret is kept out of apps/api's own request logging —
    # see that route's docstring. (The event id itself is not a secret —
    # this line is about the token, and about not needing a second
    # justification for a second location.)
    #
    # NOT A COMPLETE FIX BY ITSELF — found running this for real, not
    # assumed: `auth_request` logs "auth request unexpected status" at
    # ERROR level for any subrequest status outside 2xx/401/403 (an API
    # 500, a route drift, an upstream timeout), and nginx's error-log
    # handler independently appends the FULL request line — including
    # `?token=...` — regardless of this `access_log off`. Reproduced
    # directly: pointing the internal location at a stub that always
    # returns 500 produces exactly
    #   "auth request unexpected status: 500 ... request: \"GET
    #    /live/<eventId>/index.m3u8?token=<TOKEN> HTTP/1.1\""
    # in nginx's ERROR log. DELIBERATE CHOICE, not an oversight: this
    # project keeps nginx's error log at its default level rather than
    # raising the threshold to suppress it, because that log is exactly
    # what an operator needs when many viewers break at once (an unhealthy
    # apps/api, a resolver failure, an upstream timeout) — losing it to
    # protect one query parameter is the wrong trade. Consequence, stated
    # plainly rather than left implicit: **nginx's error log for this
    # server must be treated as a secret-bearing file** — the same
    # handling `apps/api/.env` already gets (restricted permissions, never
    # shipped to a less-trusted log aggregator without stripping query
    # strings first, not just "another log file"). This is not only the
    # watch token: the `/whip/` location above logs the STREAM KEY to this
    # same error log on its own most ordinary failure (MediaMTX
    # unreachable) — see that location's own comment. One file, two
    # different secrets, same handling required for both.
    access_log off;

    # THE REWRITE: proxy to MediaMTX's INTERNAL path
    # (`/live/<streamKey><rest>`), built from `$mtx_key` (the auth
    # subrequest's response header, NEVER from anything in the original
    # client request), not to the public path nginx received. `$is_args`
    # and `$args` re-attach the original query string (the `?token=...`
    # `hls.js`/native HLS attaches to every request — see WatchPage.tsx) —
    # supplying an explicit URI in `proxy_pass` stops nginx's usual
    # "forward the matched URI automatically" behaviour, so the query
    # string has to be re-added explicitly or every proxied request would
    # silently lose its token.
    proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8888/live/$mtx_key$mtx_rest$is_args$args;

    # `proxy_redirect` — FOUND RUNNING THIS FOR REAL, NOT ANTICIPATED FROM
    # THE DESIGN: MediaMTX answers a stream's FIRST HLS request with its own
    # `302` "cookie check" redirect (see the `Set-Cookie: cookieCheck=1`
    # dance CONTRIBUTING.md already documents) — and that redirect's
    # `Location` header is MediaMTX echoing back the REQUEST PATH IT
    # RECEIVED, which, after the rewrite above, is the INTERNAL
    # `/live/<streamKey>/...` path. Without this line, that `Location`
    # reaches the member's browser verbatim — reintroducing the exact leak
    # this whole fix exists to close, through a response HEADER instead of
    # the response body. Reproduced directly: with this line absent, `curl
    # -D -` against a fresh session showed
    #   `Location: http://<host>/live/<REAL STREAM KEY>/index.m3u8?cookieCheck=1&token=...`
    # — the browser would have followed it and the address bar / network
    # tab would show the stream key exactly as before this fix. With this
    # line present, the SAME request's `Location` reads
    #   `Location: http://<host>/live/<EVENT ID>/index.m3u8?cookieCheck=1&token=...`
    # — rewritten back to the public form before nginx ever sends it
    # onward. `$mtx_key`/`$mtx_event` are the SAME two variables the
    # request-side rewrite above already uses, so this can never drift out
    # of sync with it. (MediaMTX's sub-manifest URIs themselves — segment
    # and part filenames — are RELATIVE, with no `/live/<key>/` prefix at
    # all, so they need no equivalent rewrite: a relative URI resolves
    # against whatever path the BROWSER already sees, which is always the
    # public `/live/<eventId>/...` one. Only this one absolute redirect
    # needed handling.)
    proxy_redirect /live/$mtx_key/ /live/$mtx_event/;
    proxy_set_header Host $host;
    proxy_http_version 1.1;
    proxy_set_header Connection "";
}

# `internal;` — reachable only via `auth_request` above, never directly
# from outside this nginx (matching apps/api's own security note: the
# actual /webhooks/mediamtx/auth-request endpoint this calls must never be
# on the public surface either, and isn't — this subrequest reaches it over
# 127.0.0.1, the same private path MediaMTX's own webhook calls already
# use).
#
# Both authorisation inputs travel as HEADERS, not query parameters — the
# obvious `?eventId=...&token=...` on this subrequest's own URL does not
# work: nginx's `auth_request` subrequest does NOT inherit `$args`/`$arg_*`
# from the request it is authorising. Confirmed with a probe upstream that
# echoed back exactly what the subrequest received: `$arg_token`, read
# directly inside THIS location, came back EMPTY every time, while
# `$request_uri` and the OUTER location's own captures (`$mtx_event` above)
# both came back correct. The working pattern: capture the token into a
# plain `set $watch_token $arg_token;` in the OUTER location, BEFORE
# `auth_request` fires — a `set` variable persists into the subrequest the
# same way a regex/`if` capture does, even though `$arg_token` itself does
# not. See task-9-report.md for the full empirical trace, including the
# config that does not work.
location = /_internal/mediamtx-auth-request {
    internal;
    access_log off;

    # Bounded, rather than nginx's 60s defaults — an unhealthy apps/api
    # should fail THIS request fast (denying the segment) rather than
    # holding every in-flight viewer's connection open for a minute each.
    # Not tuned against real traffic; picked to be clearly faster than a
    # human notices a stall as "broken" without being so tight that an
    # ordinary GC pause or a briefly slow query becomes a false refusal.
    proxy_connect_timeout 3s;
    proxy_read_timeout 3s;

    proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:3000/webhooks/mediamtx/auth-request;
    proxy_pass_request_body off;
    proxy_set_header Content-Length "";
    proxy_set_header X-Mediamtx-Secret "${MEDIAMTX_WEBHOOK_SECRET}";
    # FIX 1: was `X-Mtx-Path: "live/$mtx_key"` (the streamKey-shaped
    # internal path) — apps/api now resolves reads by EVENT ID (see
    # `AuthoriseStream.authoriseReadByEventId`), so the header this
    # subrequest sends changed to match what it can actually vouch for:
    # the event id nginx captured from the PUBLIC request, nothing this
    # nginx has independently verified as a real stream key.
    proxy_set_header X-Mtx-Event-Id "$mtx_event";
    proxy_set_header X-Watch-Token "$watch_token";
}
